        ['(a) Distinguish between fundamental and derived quantities, giving three examples of each with their SI units. (b) Use dimensions to show that the equation v² = u² + 2as is dimensionally correct.', ['Definitions', 'Three fundamental with units', 'Three derived with units', 'Dimensions of each term', 'Conclusion'], '(a) Fundamental quantities are independent and not defined in terms of others: mass (kg), length (m), time (s), current (A), temperature (K). Derived quantities are obtained from fundamental ones: velocity (m/s), force (N), density (kg/m³), energy (J). (b) [v²] = [u²] = (LT⁻¹)² = L²T⁻². [2as] = LT⁻² × L = L²T⁻². All terms have dimensions L²T⁻², so the equation is homogeneous.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe how you would determine the density of an irregular stone. State two precautions.', ['Find mass', 'Displacement method', 'Volume = V₂ − V₁', 'Density formula', 'Two precautions'], 'Weigh the stone on a balance to find its mass m. Pour water into a measuring cylinder and read the volume V₁. Tie the stone with thread and lower it gently until fully submerged; read the new volume V₂. Volume of stone = V₂ − V₁. Density = m ÷ (V₂ − V₁). Precautions: avoid parallax by reading the bottom of the meniscus at eye level; make sure no air bubbles cling to the stone; lower it gently to avoid splashing.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe how a micrometer screw gauge is used to measure the diameter of a wire, and explain how its zero error is corrected.', ['Check zero error', 'Use ratchet', 'Main + thimble reading', 'Average several points', 'Correct zero error'], 'Close the jaws gently using the ratchet and check whether the thimble zero coincides with the main scale line (zero error). Open the jaws, place the wire between the anvil and spindle, and turn the ratchet until it clicks. Read the main scale (mm and half-mm) and add the thimble reading × 0.01 mm. Take readings at several points and average them. If there is a positive zero error, subtract it from the reading; if negative, add it.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain the terms accuracy, precision and significant figures. A student measures the length of a rod as 25.4 cm, 25.5 cm and 25.3 cm. Find the mean length and comment on the precision.', ['Accuracy', 'Precision', 'Significant figures', 'Mean 25.4 cm', 'Comment'], 'Accuracy is how close a measurement is to the true value. Precision is how close repeated measurements are to each other, and the smallest division of the instrument. Significant figures show the precision with which a value is known. Mean = (25.4 + 25.5 + 25.3) ÷ 3 = 25.4 cm. The readings vary by only ±0.1 cm, so the measurements are precise (reading to 1 mm with a metre rule).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Derive the dimensions of (a) pressure, (b) power, (c) momentum, and hence state their SI units in base units.', ['Pressure dimensions', 'Power dimensions', 'Momentum dimensions', 'Base units'], '(a) Pressure = force ÷ area = MLT⁻² ÷ L² = ML⁻¹T⁻²; unit kg m⁻¹ s⁻² (Pa). (b) Power = work ÷ time = ML²T⁻² ÷ T = ML²T⁻³; unit kg m² s⁻³ (W). (c) Momentum = mass × velocity = MLT⁻¹; unit kg m s⁻¹ (N s).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
    ],
