        ['Describe how the fixed points of a mercury-in-glass thermometer are determined and how the thermometer is calibrated.', ['Pure melting ice', 'Mark 0 °C', 'Steam at standard pressure', 'Mark 100 °C', 'Divide into 100 parts'], 'Lower fixed point: place the bulb in pure melting ice in a funnel so that water drains away; when the mercury level is steady, mark it as 0 °C. Upper fixed point: place the thermometer in a hypsometer so that the bulb is in the steam above boiling water at standard pressure (not in the water); when steady, mark the level as 100 °C. The distance between the marks is divided into 100 equal divisions, each 1 °C.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Draw a clinical thermometer and explain the function of (a) the constriction, (b) the narrow bore, (c) the thin glass bulb, (d) the curved stem.', ['Labelled diagram', 'Constriction', 'Narrow bore', 'Thin bulb', 'Magnifying stem'], 'Labelled diagram with bulb, constriction, capillary bore, stem and scale 35–42 °C. (a) The constriction stops the mercury flowing back into the bulb when removed from the patient, so the reading can be taken. (b) The narrow bore makes the thermometer sensitive: a small temperature change produces a large movement of mercury. (c) The thin glass bulb conducts heat quickly so the mercury reaches body temperature fast. (d) The curved front acts as a magnifying lens so the thin thread is easily read.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Compare mercury and alcohol as thermometric liquids, giving three advantages of each.', ['Three mercury advantages', 'Three alcohol advantages', 'Correct properties'], 'Mercury: opaque and easily seen; does not wet glass; good conductor of heat, so responds quickly; expands uniformly; wide range up to 357 °C. Alcohol: low freezing point (−115 °C) for very cold temperatures; expands more per degree so it is more sensitive; cheaper and less toxic; can be coloured to be seen.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['The length of the mercury column of a thermometer is 3.0 cm at 0 °C and 23.0 cm at 100 °C. (a) Find the temperature when the length is 11.0 cm. (b) Find the length when the temperature is 65 °C. (c) Explain what is meant by a thermometric property.', ['Formula', '40 °C', '16.0 cm', 'Thermometric property defined'], '(a) θ = (11.0 − 3.0) ÷ (23.0 − 3.0) × 100 = 8 ÷ 20 × 100 = 40 °C. (b) l = 3.0 + 65 ÷ 100 × 20 = 3.0 + 13.0 = 16.0 cm. (c) A thermometric property is a physical property that changes continuously and measurably with temperature, such as the length of a liquid column, electrical resistance or gas pressure.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe the thermocouple thermometer and give two advantages it has over a mercury thermometer.', ['Two different metals', 'Two junctions', 'E.m.f. measured', 'Two advantages'], 'A thermocouple consists of two wires of different metals (for example copper and iron) joined at two junctions. One junction is kept at a reference temperature (in melting ice) and the other is placed where the temperature is to be measured. A temperature difference produces an e.m.f. that is measured with a millivoltmeter calibrated in °C. Advantages: wide range (up to over 1,000 °C); quick response because the junction has small heat capacity; can measure temperature at a small spot; can be read remotely.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
