        ['Draw ray diagrams to show the image formed by a concave mirror when the object is (a) beyond C, (b) between F and the mirror. State the nature of the image in each case.', ['Diagram (a)', 'Nature (a)', 'Diagram (b)', 'Nature (b)'], '(a) A ray parallel to the axis reflects through F; a ray through C reflects back along itself; they meet between C and F. The image is real, inverted and diminished, formed between C and F. (b) A ray parallel to the axis reflects through F; a ray from the top of the object travelling along a radius (as if from C) reflects back along itself; the reflected rays diverge, and when extended behind the mirror they meet at the image. The image is virtual, upright and magnified, behind the mirror.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['An object 4 cm tall stands 30 cm from a concave mirror of focal length 20 cm. (a) Calculate the image distance. (b) Calculate the magnification and image height. (c) State the nature of the image.', ['Mirror formula', 'v = 60 cm', 'm = 2', '8 cm', 'Nature'], '(a) 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/20 − 1/30 = 1/60, so v = 60 cm (in front of the mirror). (b) m = v ÷ u = 60 ÷ 30 = 2; image height = 2 × 4 = 8 cm. (c) Real, inverted and magnified.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe an experiment to verify the laws of reflection using a plane mirror and optical pins.', ['Mirror and normal', 'Pins on incident ray', 'Align with images', 'Measure angles', 'i = r conclusion'], 'Draw a line on paper and place the mirror upright along it. Draw a normal and a line at a known angle of incidence, say 30°. Stick two pins on the incident line. Looking into the mirror, stick two more pins in line with the images of the first two. Join these pins to the mirror to get the reflected ray and measure the angle of reflection. Repeat for other angles. The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence each time, and all rays lie on the plane of the paper.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Give three uses each of concave mirrors and convex mirrors, and explain why each type suits its use.', ['Three concave uses', 'Reason', 'Three convex uses', 'Reason'], 'Concave: shaving and make-up mirrors (magnified upright image when close); reflectors in torches and headlamps (lamp at focus gives a parallel beam); solar cookers and concentrators (focus sunlight); dentists\' mirrors. Convex: driving (rear-view) mirrors (wide field of view, upright image); security mirrors in shops and at sharp road bends (wide view); street lamp reflectors (spread light).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain, with a diagram, why a plane mirror needs to be only half the height of a person for them to see their full image.', ['Diagram with rays from head and feet', 'Law of reflection', 'Halfway points', 'Half height independent of distance'], 'Light from the top of the head and from the feet reflects off the mirror into the eye. By the law of reflection, the ray from the feet strikes the mirror at a point halfway between the eye and the feet, and the ray from the top of the head strikes halfway between the eye and the top. The mirror therefore needs to extend only from halfway between eye and feet to halfway between eye and head, which is half the person\'s height, wherever they stand.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
    ],
    'SHS 1: Refraction and total internal reflection' => [
        ['Refraction is the', ['bending of light as it passes from one medium to another', 'bouncing of light off a surface', 'splitting of white light', 'absorption of light'], 'A', 'Speed changes.', 1],
        ['When light passes from air into glass it bends', ['away from the normal', 'towards the normal', 'along the surface', 'not at all'], 'B', 'It slows down.', 1],
        ['Snell\'s law states that', ['i = r', 'sin i × sin r = 1', 'sin i ÷ sin r is constant for two given media', 'i + r = 90°'], 'C', 'The constant is the refractive index.', 2],
        ['The refractive index of a medium is also equal to', ['speed in medium ÷ speed in vacuum', 'wavelength × frequency', 'density of medium', 'speed of light in vacuum ÷ speed of light in the medium'], 'D', 'n = c ÷ v.', 3],
        ['Total internal reflection occurs when light travels', ['from a denser to a less dense medium at an angle greater than the critical angle', 'from air into glass', 'along the normal', 'at any angle in air'], 'A', 'All light reflects.', 2],
        ['The critical angle C is related to refractive index n by', ['sin C = n', 'sin C = 1/n', 'tan C = n', 'cos C = 1/n'], 'B', 'Refraction angle is 90°.', 3],
        ['Optical fibres work by', ['refraction only', 'dispersion', 'total internal reflection', 'diffraction'], 'C', 'Light stays inside the fibre.', 2],
        ['A swimming pool appears shallower than it really is because of', ['reflection', 'dispersion', 'diffraction', 'refraction'], 'D', 'Apparent depth.', 1],
        ['Real depth ÷ apparent depth equals', ['the refractive index', 'the critical angle', 'one', 'the speed of light'], 'A', 'For viewing from above.', 3],
        ['A mirage on a hot road is caused by', ['reflection from water', 'refraction and total internal reflection in layers of hot air', 'dispersion', 'diffraction'], 'B', 'Hot air is less dense.', 3],
        ['Find the critical angle for a medium of refractive index 2.42, to 1 decimal place.', ['65.6°', '22.4°', '24.4°', '37.2°'], 'C', 'sin C = 1 ÷ 2.42.', 3],
        ['A ray of light in air strikes glass at an angle of incidence of 40° and is refracted at 19°. Find the refractive index of the glass, to 2 decimal places.', ['0.51', '2.11', '0.81', '1.97'], 'D', 'n = sin 40° ÷ sin 19°.', 3],
        ['Find the critical angle for a medium of refractive index 1.33, to 1 decimal place.', ['48.8°', '41.2°', '40.8°', '67.7°'], 'A', 'sin C = 1 ÷ 1.33.', 3],
        ['A ray of light in air strikes glass at an angle of incidence of 30° and is refracted at 28°. Find the refractive index of the glass, to 2 decimal places.', ['0.94', '1.07', '1.27', '0.98'], 'B', 'n = sin 30° ÷ sin 28°.', 3],
        ['A ray of light in air strikes glass at an angle of incidence of 50° and is refracted at 19°. Find the refractive index of the glass, to 2 decimal places.', ['0.42', '2.63', '2.35', '0.68'], 'C', 'n = sin 50° ÷ sin 19°.', 3],
        ['A coin lies at the bottom of a tank of liquid 6 m deep. If the refractive index of the liquid is 1.5, find the apparent depth, to 2 decimal places.', ['9 m', '4.5 m', '0.25 m', '4 m'], 'D', 'Apparent depth = real depth ÷ n.', 3],
        ['A ray of light in air strikes glass at an angle of incidence of 45° and is refracted at 22°. Find the refractive index of the glass, to 2 decimal places.', ['1.89', '0.53', '2.05', '0.76'], 'A', 'n = sin 45° ÷ sin 22°.', 3],
        ['A coin lies at the bottom of a tank of liquid 4 m deep. If the refractive index of the liquid is 1.33, find the apparent depth, to 2 decimal places.', ['5.32 m', '3.01 m', '2.67 m', '0.33 m'], 'B', 'Apparent depth = real depth ÷ n.', 3],
        ['A ray of light in air strikes glass at an angle of incidence of 60° and is refracted at 22°. Find the refractive index of the glass, to 2 decimal places.', ['0.43', '2.73', '2.31', '0.54'], 'C', 'n = sin 60° ÷ sin 22°.', 3],
        ['Find the critical angle for a medium of refractive index 1.5, to 1 decimal place.', ['48.2°', '36°', '60°', '41.8°'], 'D', 'sin C = 1 ÷ 1.5.', 3],
        ['Light travels faster in glass than in air.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It travels slower in glass.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Diamond sparkles because of its small critical angle.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'About 24°, so much total internal reflection.', 4, 'tf'],
        ['A ray travelling along the normal is not bent.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'i = 0, r = 0.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Total internal reflection can happen when light goes from air into water.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It needs light going from denser to less dense.', 2, 'tf'],
