        ['A ray of light passes from air into a glass block of refractive index 1.5 at an angle of incidence of 40°. (a) State Snell\'s law. (b) Calculate the angle of refraction. (c) Calculate the critical angle of the glass. (d) Explain, with a diagram, what happens to a ray inside the glass that strikes the glass–air surface at an angle greater than the critical angle.', ['Snell\'s law', 'r = 25.4°', 'C = 41.8°', 'Total internal reflection described'], '(a) The ratio sin i ÷ sin r is constant for light passing between two given media; the incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. (b) sin r = sin 40° ÷ 1.5 = 0.43, so r = 25.4°. (c) sin C = 1 ÷ 1.5 = 0.67, so C = 41.8°. (d) The ray is totally internally reflected back into the glass; no light escapes. The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A ray of light passes from air into a glass block of refractive index 1.6 at an angle of incidence of 40°. (a) State Snell\'s law. (b) Calculate the angle of refraction. (c) Calculate the critical angle of the glass. (d) Explain, with a diagram, what happens to a ray inside the glass that strikes the glass–air surface at an angle greater than the critical angle.', ['Snell\'s law', 'r = 23.7°', 'C = 38.7°', 'Total internal reflection described'], '(a) The ratio sin i ÷ sin r is constant for light passing between two given media; the incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. (b) sin r = sin 40° ÷ 1.6 = 0.40, so r = 23.7°. (c) sin C = 1 ÷ 1.6 = 0.63, so C = 38.7°. (d) The ray is totally internally reflected back into the glass; no light escapes. The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A ray of light passes from air into a glass block of refractive index 1.52 at an angle of incidence of 30°. (a) State Snell\'s law. (b) Calculate the angle of refraction. (c) Calculate the critical angle of the glass. (d) Explain, with a diagram, what happens to a ray inside the glass that strikes the glass–air surface at an angle greater than the critical angle.', ['Snell\'s law', 'r = 19.2°', 'C = 41.1°', 'Total internal reflection described'], '(a) The ratio sin i ÷ sin r is constant for light passing between two given media; the incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. (b) sin r = sin 30° ÷ 1.52 = 0.33, so r = 19.2°. (c) sin C = 1 ÷ 1.52 = 0.66, so C = 41.1°. (d) The ray is totally internally reflected back into the glass; no light escapes. The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A ray of light passes from air into a glass block of refractive index 1.52 at an angle of incidence of 50°. (a) State Snell\'s law. (b) Calculate the angle of refraction. (c) Calculate the critical angle of the glass. (d) Explain, with a diagram, what happens to a ray inside the glass that strikes the glass–air surface at an angle greater than the critical angle.', ['Snell\'s law', 'r = 30.3°', 'C = 41.1°', 'Total internal reflection described'], '(a) The ratio sin i ÷ sin r is constant for light passing between two given media; the incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. (b) sin r = sin 50° ÷ 1.52 = 0.50, so r = 30.3°. (c) sin C = 1 ÷ 1.52 = 0.66, so C = 41.1°. (d) The ray is totally internally reflected back into the glass; no light escapes. The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A ray of light passes from air into a glass block of refractive index 1.5 at an angle of incidence of 50°. (a) State Snell\'s law. (b) Calculate the angle of refraction. (c) Calculate the critical angle of the glass. (d) Explain, with a diagram, what happens to a ray inside the glass that strikes the glass–air surface at an angle greater than the critical angle.', ['Snell\'s law', 'r = 30.7°', 'C = 41.8°', 'Total internal reflection described'], '(a) The ratio sin i ÷ sin r is constant for light passing between two given media; the incident ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. (b) sin r = sin 50° ÷ 1.5 = 0.51, so r = 30.7°. (c) sin C = 1 ÷ 1.5 = 0.67, so C = 41.8°. (d) The ray is totally internally reflected back into the glass; no light escapes. The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
