        ['The heat needed to warm 3 kg of water by 20 K (c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) is ___ J.', ['252,000', '252000'], '', 'Q = mcΔθ.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['A metal block of mass 0.25 kg at 100 °C is dropped into 0.3 kg of water at 30 °C in a container of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature of the mixture is 40.6 °C. (Specific heat capacity of water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) (a) State the principle used. (b) Calculate the heat gained by the water. (c) Calculate the specific heat capacity of the metal. (d) State two precautions in this experiment.', ['Principle of mixtures', 'Heat gained by water', 'c of metal', 'Two precautions'], '(a) Heat lost by the hot body = heat gained by the cold body (no heat losses). (b) Heat gained = 0.3 × 4,200 × (40.6 − 30) = 13,356 J. (c) 0.25 × c × (100 − 40.6) = 13,356, so c ≈ 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (d) Transfer the metal quickly to reduce heat loss; stir the water; use a lagged calorimeter with a lid; read the highest temperature reached.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A metal block of mass 0.25 kg at 100 °C is dropped into 0.4 kg of water at 20 °C in a container of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature of the mixture is 24.5 °C. (Specific heat capacity of water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) (a) State the principle used. (b) Calculate the heat gained by the water. (c) Calculate the specific heat capacity of the metal. (d) State two precautions in this experiment.', ['Principle of mixtures', 'Heat gained by water', 'c of metal', 'Two precautions'], '(a) Heat lost by the hot body = heat gained by the cold body (no heat losses). (b) Heat gained = 0.4 × 4,200 × (24.5 − 20) = 7,560 J. (c) 0.25 × c × (100 − 24.5) = 7,560, so c ≈ 400 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (d) Transfer the metal quickly to reduce heat loss; stir the water; use a lagged calorimeter with a lid; read the highest temperature reached.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A metal block of mass 0.1 kg at 100 °C is dropped into 0.4 kg of water at 20 °C in a container of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature of the mixture is 24.1 °C. (Specific heat capacity of water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) (a) State the principle used. (b) Calculate the heat gained by the water. (c) Calculate the specific heat capacity of the metal. (d) State two precautions in this experiment.', ['Principle of mixtures', 'Heat gained by water', 'c of metal', 'Two precautions'], '(a) Heat lost by the hot body = heat gained by the cold body (no heat losses). (b) Heat gained = 0.4 × 4,200 × (24.1 − 20) = 6,888 J. (c) 0.1 × c × (100 − 24.1) = 6,888, so c ≈ 910 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (d) Transfer the metal quickly to reduce heat loss; stir the water; use a lagged calorimeter with a lid; read the highest temperature reached.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A metal block of mass 0.1 kg at 100 °C is dropped into 0.2 kg of water at 20 °C in a container of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature of the mixture is 24.1 °C. (Specific heat capacity of water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) (a) State the principle used. (b) Calculate the heat gained by the water. (c) Calculate the specific heat capacity of the metal. (d) State two precautions in this experiment.', ['Principle of mixtures', 'Heat gained by water', 'c of metal', 'Two precautions'], '(a) Heat lost by the hot body = heat gained by the cold body (no heat losses). (b) Heat gained = 0.2 × 4,200 × (24.1 − 20) = 3,444 J. (c) 0.1 × c × (100 − 24.1) = 3,444, so c ≈ 450 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (d) Transfer the metal quickly to reduce heat loss; stir the water; use a lagged calorimeter with a lid; read the highest temperature reached.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A metal block of mass 0.1 kg at 100 °C is dropped into 0.3 kg of water at 20 °C in a container of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature of the mixture is 22.5 °C. (Specific heat capacity of water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) (a) State the principle used. (b) Calculate the heat gained by the water. (c) Calculate the specific heat capacity of the metal. (d) State two precautions in this experiment.', ['Principle of mixtures', 'Heat gained by water', 'c of metal', 'Two precautions'], '(a) Heat lost by the hot body = heat gained by the cold body (no heat losses). (b) Heat gained = 0.3 × 4,200 × (22.5 − 20) = 3,150 J. (c) 0.1 × c × (100 − 22.5) = 3,150, so c ≈ 410 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (d) Transfer the metal quickly to reduce heat loss; stir the water; use a lagged calorimeter with a lid; read the highest temperature reached.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
