        ['Describe an experiment to verify Boyle\'s law. State the precautions and sketch the graph you would expect.', ['Trapped air apparatus', 'Vary pressure', 'Record P and V', 'P vs 1/V straight line', 'Precautions'], 'Trap a column of dry air in a tube with a pressure gauge (or use a J-tube with mercury). Change the pressure using a pump or by adding mercury, waiting for the temperature to return to room temperature each time. Record the pressure P and the length of the air column (proportional to volume V). Plot P against 1/V: a straight line through the origin shows P ∝ 1/V at constant temperature (PV = constant). Precautions: change the pressure slowly so that temperature stays constant; avoid parallax; ensure no air leaks.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain, using the kinetic theory, (a) why a gas exerts pressure, (b) why pressure increases when a gas is heated at constant volume, (c) why pressure increases when volume decreases at constant temperature.', ['Collisions cause pressure', 'Faster molecules when heated', 'More frequent harder collisions', 'More collisions in smaller volume'], '(a) Gas molecules move randomly and collide with the walls of the container; each collision changes their momentum, exerting a force; the force per unit area is the pressure. (b) Heating gives the molecules more kinetic energy, so they move faster, hit the walls more often and with greater force, increasing pressure. (c) In a smaller volume, the molecules hit the walls more often (more collisions per unit area per second), so pressure increases.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['A fixed mass of gas has a volume of 200 cm³ at 27 °C and 750 mmHg. Calculate its volume at 127 °C and 600 mmHg. State the law used.', ['Law stated', 'Convert to kelvin', 'Substitution', 'V₂ = 333.3 cm³'], 'The general gas law: P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂. T₁ = 300 K, T₂ = 400 K. V₂ = P₁V₁T₂ ÷ (T₁P₂) = 750 × 200 × 400 ÷ (300 × 600) = 333.3 cm³.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe how the pressure law can be used to estimate absolute zero, and explain the meaning of absolute zero.', ['Constant volume apparatus', 'Pressure vs temperature graph', 'Extrapolate to P = 0', 'About −273 °C', 'Meaning of absolute zero'], 'A flask of air connected to a pressure gauge is placed in a water bath. The water is heated and the pressure is recorded at several temperatures, keeping the volume constant. A graph of pressure against temperature in °C is a straight line. Extending (extrapolating) it to zero pressure gives an intercept on the temperature axis at about −273 °C. This is absolute zero: the lowest possible temperature, at which an ideal gas would exert no pressure and molecular motion is minimum. It is 0 K on the Kelvin scale.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['A bicycle tyre is pumped to 300 kPa at 17 °C. After riding in the sun the temperature of the air in the tyre is 47 °C. (a) Assuming the volume is constant, find the new pressure. (b) Explain why tyre pressure should be checked when tyres are cold.', ['Pressure law', 'Kelvin conversion', '331 kPa', 'Reason for checking cold'], '(a) P₂ = P₁ × T₂ ÷ T₁ = 300 × 320 ÷ 290 = 331 kPa. (b) Pressure rises as the air warms; a reading taken when hot would be higher than the true cold pressure, leading to under-inflation, or a tyre filled to the limit when cold could burst when hot.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Wave properties and the wave equation' => [
        ['A wave transfers', ['matter only', 'both energy and matter permanently', 'nothing', 'energy without transferring matter'], 'D', 'Particles oscillate about fixed points.', 1],
        ['In a transverse wave, the particles vibrate', ['perpendicular to the direction of travel', 'parallel to the direction of travel', 'in circles only', 'not at all'], 'A', 'For example light and water waves.', 1],
        ['In a longitudinal wave, the particles vibrate', ['perpendicular to the direction of travel', 'parallel to the direction of travel', 'only up and down', 'randomly'], 'B', 'For example sound.', 1],
        ['The wavelength is the distance between', ['a crest and the next trough', 'the source and the receiver', 'two successive crests (or troughs)', 'two nodes and an antinode'], 'C', 'Symbol λ.', 1],
        ['The amplitude of a wave is', ['the distance between crests', 'the number of waves per second', 'the speed of the wave', 'the maximum displacement from the rest position'], 'D', 'Related to energy.', 1],
        ['The frequency of a wave is', ['the number of complete oscillations per second', 'the time for one oscillation', 'the distance between crests', 'the height of a wave'], 'A', 'Unit hertz.', 1],
        ['The period T is related to frequency f by', ['T = f', 'T = 1/f', 'T = 2f', 'T = f²'], 'B', 'Time for one cycle.', 2],
        ['The wave equation is', ['v = f/λ', 'v = λ/f', 'v = fλ', 'v = f + λ'], 'C', 'Speed = frequency × wavelength.', 1],
        ['Diffraction is the', ['bending of waves on entering a new medium', 'bouncing back of waves', 'loss of energy of waves', 'spreading of waves through a gap or around an obstacle'], 'D', 'Greatest when gap ≈ wavelength.', 2],
        ['Interference occurs when', ['two waves overlap and combine', 'a wave reflects', 'a wave slows down', 'a wave is absorbed'], 'A', 'Constructive or destructive.', 2],
        ['Polarisation is possible only for', ['longitudinal waves', 'transverse waves', 'sound waves', 'all waves'], 'B', 'Evidence light is transverse.', 3],
        ['In a stationary wave, the points of zero displacement are called', ['antinodes', 'crests', 'nodes', 'troughs'], 'C', 'Spaced λ/2 apart.', 2],
        ['A wave has a period of 0.02 s. Find its frequency.', ['0.02 Hz', '0.2 Hz', '100 Hz', '50 Hz'], 'D', 'f = 1 ÷ T.', 1],
        ['A wave has a period of 0.05 s. Find its frequency.', ['20 Hz', '0.05 Hz', '0.5 Hz', '40 Hz'], 'A', 'f = 1 ÷ T.', 1],
        ['A wave has frequency 10 Hz and wavelength 0.5 m. Find its speed.', ['20 m/s', '5 m/s', '0.05 m/s', '10.5 m/s'], 'B', 'v = fλ.', 1],
        ['A wave has frequency 10 Hz and wavelength 0.34 m. Find its speed.', ['29.41 m/s', '0.034 m/s', '3.4 m/s', '10.34 m/s'], 'C', 'v = fλ.', 1],
