        ['A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 4 m. Find its speed.', ['200 m/s', '12.5 m/s', '0.08 m/s', '54 m/s'], 'A', 'v = fλ.', 1],
        ['A wave has a period of 0.1 s. Find its frequency.', ['0.1 Hz', '10 Hz', '1 Hz', '20 Hz'], 'B', 'f = 1 ÷ T.', 1],
        ['A wave has a period of 0.5 s. Find its frequency.', ['0.5 Hz', '5 Hz', '2 Hz', '4 Hz'], 'C', 'f = 1 ÷ T.', 1],
        ['Sound is a transverse wave.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'Sound is longitudinal.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['Light waves can travel through a vacuum.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Electromagnetic waves need no medium.', 1, 'tf'],
        ['When a wave passes into a new medium, its frequency stays the same.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Speed and wavelength change.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['Diffraction is most noticeable when the gap is much larger than the wavelength.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It is greatest when the gap is about the wavelength.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['Destructive interference occurs when a crest meets a trough.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'They cancel.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The distance between adjacent nodes in a stationary wave is one wavelength.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It is half a wavelength.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['A wave of frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 0.5 m travels at ___ m/s.', ['2.5'], '', 'v = fλ.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A wave of frequency 10 Hz and wavelength 1 m travels at ___ m/s.', ['10'], '', 'v = fλ.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A wave of frequency 4 Hz and wavelength 1 m travels at ___ m/s.', ['4'], '', 'v = fλ.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A wave of frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 5 m travels at ___ m/s.', ['25'], '', 'v = fλ.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A wave of frequency 20 Hz and wavelength 1 m travels at ___ m/s.', ['20'], '', 'v = fλ.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['A wave of frequency 20 Hz and wavelength 3 m travels at ___ m/s.', ['60'], '', 'v = fλ.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['Distinguish between transverse and longitudinal waves, giving two examples of each, and draw a diagram of each showing the wavelength.', ['Transverse definition and examples', 'Longitudinal definition and examples', 'Transverse diagram with λ', 'Longitudinal diagram with λ'], 'In a transverse wave, particles vibrate at right angles to the direction of travel, forming crests and troughs; examples: light, water ripples, waves on a string, radio waves. In a longitudinal wave, particles vibrate along the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions; examples: sound, waves in a slinky spring pushed back and forth, P-waves of earthquakes. Diagrams: a sine curve with λ marked crest to crest; a series of compressions and rarefactions with λ marked compression to compression.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe the behaviour of water waves in a ripple tank when they (a) are reflected at a straight barrier, (b) pass from deep to shallow water, (c) pass through a narrow gap.', ['Reflection', 'Refraction with shorter λ', 'Diffraction', 'Gap size effect'], '(a) Waves are reflected with the angle of incidence equal to the angle of reflection; wavelength and speed are unchanged. (b) Waves slow down in shallow water, so the wavelength decreases (frequency unchanged); if they meet the boundary at an angle they change direction (refraction). (c) Waves spread out in circular arcs beyond the gap (diffraction), most noticeably when the gap width is about one wavelength.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['A radio station broadcasts at a frequency of 100 MHz. (a) Calculate the wavelength of the waves (c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s). (b) Calculate the period. (c) Explain why long-wave radio signals can be received behind hills.', ['λ = 3 m', 'T = 10⁻⁸ s', 'Diffraction explanation'], '(a) λ = c ÷ f = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 1 × 10⁸ = 3 m. (b) T = 1 ÷ f = 1 ÷ 10⁸ = 1 × 10⁻⁸ s. (c) Long waves have wavelengths comparable to the size of hills, so they diffract (bend) around them and reach receivers behind.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain how a stationary wave is formed on a stretched string and state two differences between stationary and progressive waves.', ['Two waves in opposite directions', 'Superposition', 'Nodes and antinodes', 'Two differences'], 'A wave travelling along the string reflects at the fixed end; the incident and reflected waves, with the same frequency and amplitude, travel in opposite directions and superpose to form a stationary wave with nodes and antinodes. Differences: a stationary wave does not transfer energy, while a progressive wave does; in a stationary wave the amplitude varies from zero at nodes to maximum at antinodes, while in a progressive wave all points have the same amplitude; points between adjacent nodes vibrate in phase in a stationary wave.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain constructive and destructive interference and describe how Young\'s double-slit experiment shows that light is a wave.', ['Constructive', 'Destructive', 'Two coherent slits', 'Bright and dark fringes', 'Wave conclusion'], 'When two waves meet in phase (crest on crest), their displacements add to give a larger amplitude: constructive interference. When they meet in antiphase (crest on trough), they cancel: destructive interference. In Young\'s experiment, light from a single source passes through two narrow, close slits, which act as coherent sources. On a screen, a pattern of bright and dark fringes appears where the light waves interfere constructively and destructively. Only waves can produce interference, so light is a wave.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 2: Sound waves and resonance' => [
        ['Sound travels fastest in', ['liquids', 'gases', 'a vacuum', 'solids'], 'D', 'Particles are closely packed.', 1],
