        ['Describe an experiment using a resonance tube to measure the speed of sound in air. Explain the role of end correction.', ['Apparatus', 'First resonance l₁', 'Second resonance l₂', 'v = 2f(l₂ − l₁)', 'End correction eliminated'], 'A tube open at the top is placed in a tall jar of water so that the length of the air column can be changed. A vibrating tuning fork of known frequency f is held over the open end. The tube is raised slowly from the water until the sound is loudest (first resonance); the length l₁ is measured. It is raised further until the second resonance, length l₂. Then l₂ − l₁ = λ/2, so v = 2f(l₂ − l₁). End correction: the antinode forms slightly above the open end, so l₁ + e = λ/4; using the difference l₂ − l₁ removes e.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain the terms pitch, loudness and quality of a musical note and state the physical property each depends on.', ['Pitch–frequency', 'Loudness–amplitude', 'Quality–overtones'], 'Pitch is how high or low a note sounds; it depends on frequency. Loudness is how strong a sound is to the ear; it depends on the amplitude (and intensity) of the wave. Quality (timbre) distinguishes notes of the same pitch and loudness from different instruments; it depends on the number and relative strengths of overtones (harmonics) present with the fundamental.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
        ['A ship sends an ultrasound pulse to the sea bed and receives the echo 0.8 s later. The speed of sound in sea water is 1,500 m/s. (a) Calculate the depth of the sea. (b) Explain why ultrasound rather than audible sound is used. (c) Give two other uses of ultrasound.', ['600 m', 'Halve the time', 'Reason for ultrasound', 'Two uses'], '(a) Depth = v × t ÷ 2 = 1,500 × 0.8 ÷ 2 = 600 m. (b) Ultrasound has a short wavelength, so it forms a narrow beam that diffracts little and gives precise reflections; it is also not heard by people. (c) Pre-natal scanning of the foetus; cleaning delicate objects; detecting cracks in metals; breaking kidney stones.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['With the aid of diagrams, show the first three modes of vibration of a stretched string fixed at both ends, and give the relation between the frequencies.', ['Fundamental diagram', 'Second harmonic', 'Third harmonic', 'Ratio 1 : 2 : 3'], 'Fundamental (first harmonic): one loop, length L = λ/2, f₀ = v ÷ 2L. Second harmonic: two loops, L = λ, frequency 2f₀. Third harmonic: three loops, L = 3λ/2, frequency 3f₀. Nodes are at the fixed ends. The frequencies are in the ratio 1 : 2 : 3.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain resonance with two everyday examples, and describe one situation where resonance is harmful.', ['Definition', 'Two examples', 'Harmful example'], 'Resonance occurs when a body is set into vibration by a periodic force whose frequency equals its natural frequency, producing large amplitude. Examples: tuning a radio receiver to a station; a swing pushed at the right time goes higher; the air column of a flute or a guitar body resonating. Harmful: a bridge may collapse if soldiers march in step at its natural frequency (Tacoma Narrows was set oscillating by wind); vibrations of machine parts can cause damage.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
    ],
