        ['Describe how a gold-leaf electroscope can be charged positively by induction. Explain each step with diagrams.', ['Bring negative rod near', 'Earth the cap', 'Remove earth before rod', 'Positive charge remains', 'Leaf observations'], '1. Bring a negatively charged rod near the cap of an uncharged electroscope: electrons in the cap are repelled down to the leaf and plate, which become negative and the leaf diverges; the cap becomes positive. 2. Touch (earth) the cap with a finger while the rod is near: the repelled electrons flow to earth, and the leaf collapses. 3. Remove the finger first, then remove the rod. 4. The electroscope is left with a deficit of electrons; the positive charge spreads over it and the leaf diverges again. It is now positively charged.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain how a gold-leaf electroscope can be used to (a) detect charge, (b) find the sign of a charge, (c) distinguish a conductor from an insulator.', ['Detect charge', 'Known charge and increase', 'Increase is sure test', 'Conductor vs insulator'], '(a) Bring the body near or touch the cap of an uncharged electroscope; if the leaf diverges, the body is charged. (b) Charge the electroscope with a known charge (say positive). Bring the body near: if the divergence increases, the body has the same (positive) charge; if it decreases, it is negative or uncharged. Increase of divergence is the sure test. (c) Touch the cap of a charged electroscope with the body held in the hand: if the leaf collapses quickly, the body is a conductor; if not, it is an insulator.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Three capacitors of 2 μF, 3 μF and 6 μF are connected (a) in series, (b) in parallel, across a 12 V supply. For each arrangement calculate the total capacitance and the total charge stored.', ['Series formula', '1 μF', '12 μC', 'Parallel 11 μF', '132 μC'], '(a) Series: 1/C = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 1, so C = 1 μF; Q = CV = 1 × 12 = 12 μC (the same charge on each). (b) Parallel: C = 2 + 3 + 6 = 11 μF; Q = 11 × 12 = 132 μC.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain how lightning occurs and how a lightning conductor protects a tall building.', ['Charge separation in clouds', 'Induced charge on ground', 'Air ionises', 'Pointed copper spikes', 'Safe path to earth'], 'Rubbing between moving air, water droplets and ice particles in thunderclouds separates charges, usually making the cloud base negative. This induces a positive charge on the ground and buildings below. When the electric field becomes strong enough, air is ionised and a huge discharge (lightning) occurs. A lightning conductor is a thick copper strip with sharp spikes at the top, connected to a metal plate buried in the earth. Charge concentrates at the points and ionises the air, partly neutralising the cloud; if lightning strikes, the charge flows safely through the copper to earth instead of through the building.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['State three factors affecting the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor and give three uses of capacitors.', ['Area', 'Separation', 'Dielectric', 'Three uses'], 'Factors: area of overlap of the plates (C ∝ A); distance between the plates (C ∝ 1/d); the dielectric material between the plates (permittivity ε). Uses: smoothing the output of rectifiers in power supplies; tuning circuits in radios; timing circuits; storing energy for camera flashes; blocking d.c. while passing a.c.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
    ],
