        ['A cell of e.m.f. 9 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is connected to a 4 Ω resistor in series with a parallel combination of 3 Ω and 12 Ω resistors. Calculate (a) the effective resistance of the parallel combination, (b) the total external resistance, (c) the current from the cell, (d) the terminal p.d., (e) the current in the 3 Ω resistor.', ['Parallel resistance', 'External resistance', 'Current', 'Terminal p.d.', 'Branch current'], '(a) R = 3 × 12 ÷ (3 + 12) = 2.40 Ω. (b) External = 2.40 + 4 = 6.40 Ω. (c) I = 9 ÷ (6.40 + 0.5) = 1.30 A. (d) Terminal p.d. = 1.30 × 6.40 = 8.32 V. (e) p.d. across the parallel part = 1.30 × 2.40 = 3.12 V, so the current in 3 Ω = 1.04 A.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 10],
        ['A cell of e.m.f. 9 V and internal resistance 1 Ω is connected to a 4 Ω resistor in series with a parallel combination of 3 Ω and 6 Ω resistors. Calculate (a) the effective resistance of the parallel combination, (b) the total external resistance, (c) the current from the cell, (d) the terminal p.d., (e) the current in the 3 Ω resistor.', ['Parallel resistance', 'External resistance', 'Current', 'Terminal p.d.', 'Branch current'], '(a) R = 3 × 6 ÷ (3 + 6) = 2.00 Ω. (b) External = 2.00 + 4 = 6.00 Ω. (c) I = 9 ÷ (6.00 + 1) = 1.29 A. (d) Terminal p.d. = 1.29 × 6.00 = 7.74 V. (e) p.d. across the parallel part = 1.29 × 2.00 = 2.58 V, so the current in 3 Ω = 0.86 A.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 10],
        ['A cell of e.m.f. 9 V and internal resistance 1 Ω is connected to a 2 Ω resistor in series with a parallel combination of 6 Ω and 12 Ω resistors. Calculate (a) the effective resistance of the parallel combination, (b) the total external resistance, (c) the current from the cell, (d) the terminal p.d., (e) the current in the 6 Ω resistor.', ['Parallel resistance', 'External resistance', 'Current', 'Terminal p.d.', 'Branch current'], '(a) R = 6 × 12 ÷ (6 + 12) = 4.00 Ω. (b) External = 4.00 + 2 = 6.00 Ω. (c) I = 9 ÷ (6.00 + 1) = 1.29 A. (d) Terminal p.d. = 1.29 × 6.00 = 7.74 V. (e) p.d. across the parallel part = 1.29 × 4.00 = 5.16 V, so the current in 6 Ω = 0.86 A.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 10],
        ['A cell of e.m.f. 9 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is connected to a 2 Ω resistor in series with a parallel combination of 3 Ω and 6 Ω resistors. Calculate (a) the effective resistance of the parallel combination, (b) the total external resistance, (c) the current from the cell, (d) the terminal p.d., (e) the current in the 3 Ω resistor.', ['Parallel resistance', 'External resistance', 'Current', 'Terminal p.d.', 'Branch current'], '(a) R = 3 × 6 ÷ (3 + 6) = 2.00 Ω. (b) External = 2.00 + 2 = 4.00 Ω. (c) I = 9 ÷ (4.00 + 0.5) = 2.00 A. (d) Terminal p.d. = 2.00 × 4.00 = 8.00 V. (e) p.d. across the parallel part = 2.00 × 2.00 = 4.00 V, so the current in 3 Ω = 1.33 A.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 10],
        ['A cell of e.m.f. 12 V and internal resistance 1 Ω is connected to a 2 Ω resistor in series with a parallel combination of 6 Ω and 12 Ω resistors. Calculate (a) the effective resistance of the parallel combination, (b) the total external resistance, (c) the current from the cell, (d) the terminal p.d., (e) the current in the 6 Ω resistor.', ['Parallel resistance', 'External resistance', 'Current', 'Terminal p.d.', 'Branch current'], '(a) R = 6 × 12 ÷ (6 + 12) = 4.00 Ω. (b) External = 4.00 + 2 = 6.00 Ω. (c) I = 12 ÷ (6.00 + 1) = 1.71 A. (d) Terminal p.d. = 1.71 × 6.00 = 10.26 V. (e) p.d. across the parallel part = 1.71 × 4.00 = 6.84 V, so the current in 6 Ω = 1.14 A.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 10],
