        ['Draw and label a three-pin plug and explain the function of each wire and of the fuse.', ['Labelled diagram', 'Live', 'Neutral', 'Earth', 'Fuse'], 'Labelled plug: brown live wire to the right terminal through the fuse, blue neutral wire to the left terminal, green and yellow earth wire to the top terminal, cable grip and insulated casing. Live carries current at high voltage to the appliance; neutral completes the circuit at about zero voltage; earth connects the metal casing to the ground so that if a fault makes the casing live, a large current flows to earth and the fuse blows. The fuse is a thin wire that melts if the current exceeds its rating, cutting off the live supply.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['A household uses the following each day: four 60 W bulbs for 5 hours, a 2 kW electric kettle for 30 minutes and a 150 W television for 6 hours. Calculate (a) the daily energy used in kWh, (b) the cost for 30 days at GH₵1.20 per kWh.', ['Bulbs 1.2 kWh', 'Kettle 1.0 kWh', 'TV 0.9 kWh', 'Total 3.1 kWh', 'GH₵111.60'], '(a) Bulbs: 4 × 0.06 kW × 5 h = 1.2 kWh. Kettle: 2 kW × 0.5 h = 1.0 kWh. TV: 0.15 kW × 6 h = 0.9 kWh. Total = 3.1 kWh per day. (b) For 30 days: 3.1 × 30 = 93 kWh; cost = 93 × 1.20 = GH₵111.60.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain why appliances in a house are connected in parallel rather than in series. Give three reasons.', ['Full voltage', 'Independent switching', 'Failure of one does not affect others'], 'In parallel, each appliance gets the full mains voltage (240 V), so it works at its rated power. Each appliance can be switched on and off independently without affecting the others. If one appliance fails or a bulb blows, the others continue to work. In series, the voltage would be shared and all would go off if one failed.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 5],
        ['State four safety precautions in the use of electricity at home and explain the reason for each.', ['Four precautions', 'Reasons'], 'Do not touch switches with wet hands, because water conducts and may cause electric shock. Do not overload sockets, as excessive current may overheat cables and cause fire. Use the correct fuse rating so that faults cause the fuse to blow. Replace worn or frayed cables to prevent exposed live wires. Switch off the mains before repairs. Ensure metal appliances are earthed.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
        ['Show that P = I²R and P = V²/R, starting from P = IV. A 240 V, 1,200 W iron is used. Find (a) the current, (b) its resistance, (c) the energy used in 15 minutes.', ['P = I²R derived', 'P = V²/R derived', 'I = 5 A', 'R = 48 Ω', 'E = 1.08 MJ'], 'P = IV and V = IR, so P = I(IR) = I²R; also I = V/R, so P = (V/R)V = V²/R. (a) I = P ÷ V = 1,200 ÷ 240 = 5 A. (b) R = V ÷ I = 240 ÷ 5 = 48 Ω. (c) E = Pt = 1,200 × 15 × 60 = 1,080,000 J = 1.08 MJ (0.3 kWh).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
