        ['Reversing the current in a wire reverses the direction of its magnetic field.', ['True', 'False'], 'A', 'Right-hand grip rule.', 2, 'tf'],
        ['The force on a wire parallel to a magnetic field is maximum.', ['True', 'False'], 'B', 'It is zero when parallel.', 3, 'tf'],
        ['Iron, nickel and cobalt are described as ___ materials.', ['ferromagnetic', 'magnetic'], '', 'Strongly attracted by magnets.', 1, 'fill'],
        ['The point where the magnetic field of a magnet cancels the Earth\'s field is called a ___ point.', ['neutral'], '', 'No resultant field.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The rule used to find the direction of the magnetic field around a wire is the right-hand ___ rule.', ['grip', 'screw', 'thumb'], '', 'Thumb along the current.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Small regions in a magnetic material in which atoms are aligned are called ___.', ['domains', 'magnetic domains'], '', 'Domain theory.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The angle of dip at the magnetic equator is ___°.', ['0', 'zero'], '', 'The field is horizontal.', 3, 'fill'],
        ['The SI unit of magnetic flux density is the ___.', ['tesla', 'T'], '', '1 T = 1 N A⁻¹ m⁻¹.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Describe how a steel bar can be magnetised by (a) the single-touch method, (b) the electrical method. State how the polarity is determined in each case.', ['Single-touch method', 'Polarity rule (a)', 'Solenoid with d.c.', 'Polarity rule (b)'], '(a) Stroke the steel bar from one end to the other with one pole of a magnet many times in the same direction, lifting the magnet high between strokes. The end of the bar where the stroke finishes takes the opposite polarity to the stroking pole. (b) Place the bar inside a solenoid and pass a large direct current through the coil for a short time. Polarity is given by the right-hand grip rule (or clock rule): the end where current flows anticlockwise when viewed is a north pole.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain the domain theory of magnetism and use it to explain (a) magnetisation, (b) demagnetisation by heating, (c) magnetic saturation.', ['Domains described', 'Random in unmagnetised', 'Alignment on magnetisation', 'Heating randomises', 'Saturation'], 'A ferromagnetic material contains tiny regions called domains, each a small magnet with atoms aligned. In an unmagnetised bar, domains point in random directions and their effects cancel. (a) When magnetised, the domains rotate and line up in one direction. (b) Heating gives the atoms energy so the domains vibrate and return to random orientations. (c) When all domains are aligned, the magnet cannot be made stronger: it is saturated.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Sketch the magnetic field patterns of (a) a bar magnet, (b) two like poles facing each other, (c) a long solenoid carrying current.', ['Bar magnet pattern', 'Like poles and neutral point', 'Solenoid uniform inside', 'Poles of solenoid'], '(a) Field lines leave the north pole, curve round and enter the south pole; lines are closest near the poles. (b) Lines from each pole curve away from each other, with a neutral point midway where no lines pass. (c) Inside the solenoid the lines are straight, parallel and close (uniform field); outside the pattern resembles that of a bar magnet, with poles at the ends given by the grip rule.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe the structure and action of an electric bell.', ['Parts named', 'Electromagnet attracts armature', 'Hammer strikes gong', 'Circuit broken', 'Repeats'], 'An electric bell has an electromagnet (soft iron core with coils), a soft iron armature attached to a spring and a hammer, a contact screw and a gong. When the switch is pressed, current flows through the coils; the electromagnet attracts the armature and the hammer strikes the gong. This breaks the circuit at the contact screw, so the electromagnet loses its magnetism and the spring pulls the armature back, remaking contact. The process repeats rapidly, so the bell rings continuously.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['A straight wire 0.4 m long carries a current of 5 A at right angles to a uniform magnetic field of 0.25 T. (a) Calculate the force on the wire. (b) State how the force changes if the wire is turned to 30° to the field. (c) State Fleming\'s left-hand rule.', ['F = 0.5 N', 'F = 0.25 N', 'Left-hand rule'], '(a) F = BIL = 0.25 × 5 × 0.4 = 0.5 N. (b) F = BIL sin 30° = 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25 N, so the force halves. (c) Hold the thumb, first and second fingers of the left hand mutually at right angles: the first finger points along the field, the second finger along the current, and the thumb gives the direction of the force (motion).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
