        ['A stone of mass 0.2 kg is whirled in a horizontal circle of radius 0.5 m at 4 revolutions per second. Calculate (a) its angular velocity, (b) its linear speed, (c) the centripetal acceleration, (d) the tension in the string.', ['ω = 25.13 rad/s', 'v = 12.57 m/s', 'a = 315.8 m/s²', 'T = 63.2 N'], '(a) ω = 2πf = 2π × 4 = 25.13 rad/s. (b) v = ωr = 25.13 × 0.5 = 12.57 m/s. (c) a = v² ÷ r = 12.57² ÷ 0.5 = 315.8 m/s² (or ω²r). (d) T = ma = 0.2 × 315.8 = 63.2 N.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['State Newton\'s law of universal gravitation. Show that the acceleration due to gravity at the Earth\'s surface is g = GM/R², and use it to estimate the mass of the Earth (g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m, G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²).', ['Law stated', 'mg = GMm/R²', 'g = GM/R²', 'M ≈ 6.0 × 10²⁴ kg'], 'Every particle of matter attracts every other particle with a force proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: F = GMm/r². For a mass m on the Earth\'s surface, its weight mg equals the gravitational force: mg = GMm/R², so g = GM/R². M = gR²/G = 9.8 × (6.4 × 10⁶)² ÷ 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ ≈ 6.0 × 10²⁴ kg.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain why a car may skid when going round a bend too fast and how banking the road helps.', ['Centripetal force needed', 'Friction provides it', 'Exceeds maximum friction', 'Banking component of reaction'], 'To move in a circle, the car needs a centripetal force mv²/r towards the centre, supplied on a flat road only by friction between the tyres and road. At high speed the required force exceeds the maximum friction, so the car slides outward (along a tangent) and skids. On a banked road, the normal reaction is tilted towards the centre, so its horizontal component provides part or all of the centripetal force, allowing safe higher speeds with less reliance on friction.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
        ['Describe two uses of artificial satellites and explain what is meant by a geostationary orbit.', ['Two uses', 'Above equator', 'Period 24 h', 'Same direction', 'Fixed relative to ground'], 'Uses: communication (television, telephone and internet relay); weather forecasting and monitoring; navigation (GPS); remote sensing of crops, forests and mining; scientific observation of space. A geostationary satellite orbits above the equator in the same direction as the Earth rotates, with a period of 24 hours, so it stays above the same point on the Earth\'s surface. It is at a height of about 36,000 km.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
        ['Distinguish between mass and weight. A man has a mass of 72 kg. Find his weight on the Earth (g = 10 N/kg) and on the Moon (g = 1.6 N/kg). Explain why his mass is the same in both places.', ['Mass definition', 'Weight definition', '720 N', '115.2 N', 'Mass unchanged'], 'Mass is the quantity of matter in a body, measured in kilograms with a beam balance; it is a scalar and does not change with location. Weight is the gravitational force on a body, measured in newtons with a spring balance; it is a vector and depends on g. On Earth: W = 72 × 10 = 720 N. On the Moon: W = 72 × 1.6 = 115.2 N. His mass is unchanged because the amount of matter in his body does not depend on where he is.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
    ],
