        ['Draw ray diagrams to show image formation by a converging lens when the object is (a) between F and 2F, (b) between the lens and F. Give one use for each arrangement.', ['Diagram (a)', 'Nature (a) and use', 'Diagram (b)', 'Nature (b) and use'], '(a) A ray parallel to the axis refracts through F on the other side; a ray through the optical centre goes straight; they meet beyond 2F. The image is real, inverted and magnified. Use: projector or photographic enlarger. (b) The refracted rays diverge; extended backwards they meet on the same side as the object. The image is virtual, upright and magnified. Use: magnifying glass.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Compare the human eye with a camera under the headings: focusing, control of light, image receiver and nature of image.', ['Focusing', 'Light control', 'Receiver', 'Image nature'], 'Focusing: the eye changes the shape (focal length) of its lens using ciliary muscles; the camera moves the lens towards or away from the film/sensor. Light control: the eye uses the iris to change the pupil size; the camera uses an adjustable diaphragm (aperture) and shutter speed. Image receiver: the retina in the eye; film or an electronic sensor in the camera. Image: in both, real, inverted and diminished.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain short sight and long sight, with diagrams, and state how each is corrected.', ['Short sight cause and diagram', 'Diverging correction', 'Long sight cause and diagram', 'Converging correction'], 'Short sight: a person sees near objects clearly but not distant ones, because the eyeball is too long or the lens too strong, so parallel rays focus in front of the retina. Corrected by a diverging lens, which spreads the rays so they focus on the retina. Long sight: distant objects are clear but near objects are blurred, because the eyeball is too short or the lens too weak, so rays from near objects focus behind the retina. Corrected by a converging lens.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['An object is placed 15 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. (a) Calculate the image distance. (b) Calculate the magnification. (c) State the nature of the image. (d) Calculate the power of the lens.', ['v = 30 cm', 'm = 2', 'Nature', 'P = +10 D'], '(a) 1/v = 1/10 − 1/15 = 1/30, so v = 30 cm. (b) m = v ÷ u = 30 ÷ 15 = 2. (c) Real, inverted and magnified (twice the size). (d) P = 1 ÷ 0.10 m = +10 D.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe the compound microscope and explain how it produces a highly magnified image.', ['Two converging lenses', 'Objective forms real magnified image', 'Eyepiece as magnifier', 'Final virtual image', 'Magnification multiplies'], 'It has two converging lenses: an objective of short focal length near the object, and an eyepiece of longer focal length. The object is placed just beyond the focal point of the objective, which forms a real, inverted, magnified image inside the tube. This image lies within the focal length of the eyepiece, which acts as a magnifying glass and forms a final virtual, highly magnified image. The total magnification is the product of the magnifications of the two lenses.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
