        ['Describe a simple experiment to demonstrate electromagnetic induction and state three factors that affect the size of the induced e.m.f.', ['Coil and galvanometer', 'Deflection when moving', 'No deflection when still', 'Reverse on withdrawal', 'Three factors'], 'Connect a coil of many turns to a sensitive galvanometer. Push a bar magnet into the coil: the galvanometer deflects. Hold the magnet still: no deflection. Pull it out: the galvanometer deflects in the opposite direction. Factors: the speed of movement (rate of change of flux); the number of turns on the coil; the strength of the magnet; using a soft iron core.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe the structure of a transformer and explain how it works. State three causes of energy loss and how each is reduced.', ['Structure', 'Changing flux induces e.m.f.', 'Turns ratio', 'Three losses', 'Methods of reduction'], 'A transformer has two coils, primary and secondary, wound on a laminated soft iron core. An alternating current in the primary produces a changing magnetic flux in the core, which links the secondary and induces an alternating e.m.f. in it (Vs/Vp = Ns/Np). Losses: resistance of the copper coils (I²R heating) – use thick copper wire; eddy currents in the core – laminate the core; hysteresis loss from repeated magnetisation – use soft iron; flux leakage – wind the coils on top of each other.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 10],
        ['Explain why electrical power is transmitted at high voltage. A power station sends 100 kW through cables of total resistance 5 Ω. Compare the power loss when transmitted at (a) 1,000 V, (b) 20,000 V.', ['Smaller current at high V', 'I²R loss', '(a) 50 kW', '(b) 125 W', 'Comparison'], 'For a given power P = IV, a higher voltage means a smaller current, and the power lost as heat in the cables is I²R, so losses are much smaller. (a) I = 100,000 ÷ 1,000 = 100 A; loss = 100² × 5 = 50,000 W (half the power!). (b) I = 100,000 ÷ 20,000 = 5 A; loss = 5² × 5 = 125 W. Transmission at high voltage reduces the loss by a factor of 400.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe the structure and action of a simple a.c. generator. Sketch the output voltage against time.', ['Coil, magnet, slip rings, brushes', 'Changing flux', 'Max and zero positions', 'Reverses each half turn', 'Sine wave sketch'], 'A rectangular coil is rotated between the poles of a magnet; its ends are connected to two slip rings, which press against carbon brushes connected to the external circuit. As the coil rotates, the flux linking it changes and an e.m.f. is induced (Fleming\'s right-hand rule). The e.m.f. is maximum when the coil sides cut the field lines at right angles (coil plane parallel to field) and zero when the coil is perpendicular to the field. The direction reverses every half turn, giving an alternating sinusoidal output.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['State Lenz\'s law and explain how it is a consequence of the conservation of energy. Use the example of a magnet pushed into a coil.', ['Lenz\'s law', 'Near end becomes N pole', 'Work done against repulsion', 'Energy conservation argument'], 'Lenz\'s law: the direction of the induced current is such that it opposes the change producing it. When the north pole of a magnet is pushed into a coil, the induced current makes the near end of the coil a north pole, which repels the magnet. Work must be done against this repulsion to push the magnet in, and this work is converted to electrical energy. If the induced current helped the motion, energy would be created from nothing, which is impossible.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 5],
    ],
