        ['A logic gate whose output is 0 only when both inputs are 0 is the ___ gate.', ['OR'], '', 'Truth table.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Explain the formation of the depletion layer at a p–n junction and describe the behaviour of the diode under forward and reverse bias. Sketch its I–V characteristic.', ['Diffusion forms depletion layer', 'Potential barrier', 'Forward bias conducts', 'Reverse bias blocks', 'I–V sketch'], 'When p- and n-type materials are joined, electrons diffuse from the n-side into the p-side and fill holes, while holes diffuse the other way. This leaves a thin region near the junction with no free carriers (the depletion layer) and a potential barrier. Forward bias (p to +): the barrier is reduced, the depletion layer narrows and a large current flows once about 0.6 V (for silicon) is exceeded. Reverse bias (p to −): the depletion layer widens and only a tiny leakage current flows until breakdown. The I–V graph shows almost no current in reverse and a sharp rise beyond 0.6 V in forward bias.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Distinguish between n-type and p-type semiconductors, explaining how each is made and what its majority carriers are.', ['n-type doping', 'Electrons majority', 'p-type doping', 'Holes majority', 'Neutral overall'], 'An n-type semiconductor is made by doping pure silicon with a small amount of a pentavalent element (phosphorus, arsenic). Four of its valence electrons bond with silicon and the fifth is free, so electrons are the majority carriers. A p-type semiconductor is made by doping with a trivalent element (boron, aluminium, gallium). Only three electrons bond, leaving a hole in the fourth bond, so holes are the majority carriers. In both, the material is electrically neutral overall.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Draw a circuit for full-wave bridge rectification and explain how it works. Show how a capacitor smooths the output.', ['Bridge circuit', 'First half-cycle', 'Second half-cycle', 'Same direction through load', 'Capacitor smoothing'], 'Four diodes are arranged in a bridge, with the a.c. input across one diagonal and the load across the other. During one half-cycle, two diodes are forward biased and conduct current through the load in one direction; during the other half-cycle, the other two diodes conduct, again sending current through the load in the same direction. The output is d.c. with pulses every half-cycle. A large capacitor connected across the load charges at each peak and discharges slowly through the load between peaks, smoothing the ripples.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Draw the symbols and truth tables for the AND, OR and NOT gates. Give one practical use of a logic circuit.', ['AND table', 'OR table', 'NOT table', 'Symbols', 'Use'], 'AND: output 1 only if both A and B are 1 (00→0, 01→0, 10→0, 11→1). OR: output 1 if either or both inputs are 1 (00→0, 01→1, 10→1, 11→1). NOT: output is the inverse of the input (0→1, 1→0). Uses: burglar alarms, automatic street lights (LDR with a NOT gate), car seat-belt warning systems, computers.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain how a transistor can be used as a switch to turn on a lamp automatically when it gets dark.', ['Potential divider with LDR', 'Base voltage threshold', 'Day: transistor off', 'Dark: transistor on', 'Lamp in collector circuit'], 'An LDR and a fixed resistor form a potential divider connected to the base of an n-p-n transistor; the lamp is in the collector circuit. In daylight the LDR resistance is low, so the base voltage is below about 0.6 V and the transistor is off; no collector current flows and the lamp is off. When it gets dark, the LDR resistance rises, the base voltage rises above 0.6 V, a small base current flows and switches the transistor on; a large collector current flows and the lamp lights.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
    ],
