        ['(a) Explain what is meant by mass defect and binding energy. (b) The mass defect of a helium-4 nucleus is 0.0304 u. Calculate its binding energy in MeV (1 u = 931 MeV) and the binding energy per nucleon.', ['Mass defect', 'Binding energy', '28.3 MeV', '7.07 MeV per nucleon'], '(a) Mass defect is the difference between the total mass of the separate protons and neutrons and the actual mass of the nucleus. Binding energy is the energy equivalent of the mass defect (E = Δmc²): the energy needed to separate the nucleus completely into its nucleons. (b) Binding energy = 0.0304 × 931 = 28.3 MeV. Binding energy per nucleon = 28.3 ÷ 4 = 7.07 MeV.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe the main parts of a nuclear power reactor and the function of each.', ['Fuel rods', 'Moderator', 'Control rods', 'Coolant and heat exchanger', 'Shielding'], 'Fuel rods contain enriched uranium-235, which undergoes fission and releases energy and neutrons. The moderator (graphite or water) slows the fast neutrons so they can cause further fission. Control rods of boron or cadmium absorb neutrons; they are raised or lowered to control the rate of the chain reaction. The coolant (water, CO₂) carries heat from the core to a heat exchanger where steam is made to drive turbines and generators. A thick concrete shield absorbs radiation.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe how the half-life of a short-lived radioactive source can be measured using a Geiger–Müller tube.', ['Background count', 'Fixed distance', 'Readings at intervals', 'Corrected count rate', 'Read half-life from graph'], 'First measure the background count rate with no source present. Place the source at a fixed distance from the GM tube and record the count rate at regular intervals (for example every minute). Subtract the background count from each reading to get the corrected count rate. Plot the corrected count rate against time; the curve decays exponentially. The half-life is read as the time for the count rate to fall to half its value, averaged from several points on the graph.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Discuss two advantages and two disadvantages of nuclear power compared with fossil-fuel power stations.', ['Two advantages', 'Two disadvantages', 'Examples'], 'Advantages: a very large amount of energy from a small mass of fuel; no carbon dioxide or sulphur dioxide emissions, so no contribution to global warming or acid rain; fuel reserves last a long time. Disadvantages: radioactive waste remains dangerous for thousands of years and is hard to store; risk of serious accidents releasing radiation (such as Chernobyl and Fukushima); high building and decommissioning costs; risk of nuclear weapons proliferation.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['A radioactive source has a half-life of 5 days and an initial activity of 3,200 Bq. (a) Find its activity after 20 days. (b) How long will it take for the activity to fall to 100 Bq? (c) Calculate its decay constant in s⁻¹.', ['200 Bq', '25 days', 'Convert to seconds', 'λ = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹'], '(a) 20 days = 4 half-lives: 3,200 → 1,600 → 800 → 400 → 200 Bq. (b) 3,200 ÷ 100 = 32 = 2⁵, so 5 half-lives = 25 days. (c) λ = 0.693 ÷ T½ = 0.693 ÷ (5 × 24 × 3,600 s) = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
