        ['Describe an experiment to determine the acceleration due to gravity, g, using a simple pendulum. State two precautions.', ['Apparatus', 'Measure l and time 20 swings', 'T² vs l graph', 'g = 4π²/gradient', 'Two precautions'], 'Suspend a small heavy bob on a light inextensible thread from a rigid support. Measure the length l from the point of suspension to the centre of the bob. Displace the bob through a small angle (less than 10°) and release. Time 20 complete oscillations and find the period T. Repeat for several lengths. Plot T² against l: a straight line through the origin with gradient 4π²/g, so g = 4π² ÷ gradient. Precautions: keep the amplitude small; time from the centre of the swing; ensure the bob swings in one plane; use a fiducial mark.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['A body moves with SHM of amplitude 0.05 m and period 0.2 s. Calculate (a) the angular frequency, (b) the maximum speed, (c) the maximum acceleration.', ['ω = 31.4 rad/s', 'v_max = 1.57 m/s', 'a_max = 49.3 m/s²'], '(a) ω = 2π ÷ T = 2π ÷ 0.2 = 31.4 rad/s. (b) v_max = ωA = 31.4 × 0.05 = 1.57 m/s. (c) a_max = ω²A = 31.4² × 0.05 = 49.3 m/s².', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain the energy changes during one complete oscillation of a simple pendulum and sketch graphs of kinetic and potential energy against displacement.', ['PE at extremes', 'KE at centre', 'Conversion', 'Total constant', 'Graphs'], 'At an extreme position, the bob is momentarily at rest: all its energy is gravitational potential energy. As it swings down, PE is converted to KE; at the lowest (equilibrium) point, KE is maximum and PE is minimum. As it rises to the other side, KE changes back to PE. The total energy remains constant (if undamped). Graph: PE is a U-shaped parabola with minimum at x = 0; KE is an inverted parabola with maximum at x = 0; their sum is a horizontal line.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Distinguish between free, damped and forced oscillations, giving an example of each.', ['Free and example', 'Damped and example', 'Forced and example', 'Resonance link'], 'Free oscillations occur at the natural frequency with no external force and no energy loss, so the amplitude is constant (an ideal pendulum in a vacuum). Damped oscillations lose energy to the surroundings, so the amplitude decreases with time (a pendulum in air, car shock absorbers). Forced oscillations are driven by an external periodic force at the driving frequency (a child pushed on a swing, a loudspeaker cone); if the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, resonance occurs.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['A spring stretches by 0.04 m when a mass of 0.2 kg hangs from it (g = 10 m/s²). (a) Find the spring constant. (b) Find the period of vertical oscillations of the mass. (c) Find the period if the mass is increased to 0.8 kg.', ['k = 50 N/m', 'T = 0.40 s', 'T = 0.79 s', 'Period doubles'], '(a) k = F ÷ x = 0.2 × 10 ÷ 0.04 = 50 N/m. (b) T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(0.2/50) = 0.40 s. (c) T = 2π√(0.8/50) = 0.79 s (the period doubles when the mass is multiplied by 4).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
    'SHS 3: Elasticity and Hooke\'s law' => [
        ['Hooke\'s law states that the extension of a spring is', ['inversely proportional to force', 'directly proportional to the applied force, up to the limit of proportionality', 'independent of force', 'proportional to the square of force'], 'B', 'F = kx.', 1],
        ['The elastic limit is the point beyond which', ['Hooke\'s law starts', 'the material is unstretched', 'a material does not return to its original length when the load is removed', 'the material becomes stronger'], 'C', 'Permanent deformation.', 2],
        ['Stress is defined as', ['extension per unit length', 'force × length', 'mass per unit volume', 'force per unit cross-sectional area'], 'D', 'Unit Pa.', 2],
        ['Strain is defined as', ['extension ÷ original length', 'force ÷ area', 'length × extension', 'force × extension'], 'A', 'No units.', 2],
        ['Young\'s modulus is', ['strain ÷ stress', 'stress ÷ strain', 'force ÷ extension', 'stress × strain'], 'B', 'Unit Pa.', 3],
        ['The energy stored in a stretched spring is', ['Fx', '2Fx', '½Fx', 'F/x'], 'C', 'Area under the F–x graph; also ½kx².', 3],
