        ['Describe an experiment to verify Hooke\'s law for a spiral spring. Sketch the expected graph and explain how the spring constant is obtained.', ['Apparatus', 'Load and record', 'Calculate extension', 'Straight line through origin', 'k = gradient'], 'Hang the spring from a clamp with a pointer and a metre rule beside it. Record the pointer reading with no load. Add known masses (for example 50 g at a time) and record the reading each time; calculate the extension. Also take readings while unloading to check for permanent deformation. Plot load (force) against extension: a straight line through the origin shows that extension is proportional to force (Hooke\'s law). The spring constant k is the gradient of the force–extension graph.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['A steel wire 2.0 m long with cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² stretches by 1.0 mm when a load of 100 N is applied. Calculate (a) the stress, (b) the strain, (c) the Young modulus of steel, (d) the energy stored in the wire.', ['Stress', 'Strain', 'Young modulus 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa', 'Energy 0.05 J'], '(a) Stress = F ÷ A = 100 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 1.0 × 10⁸ Pa. (b) Strain = 1.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 2.0 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴. (c) E = stress ÷ strain = 1.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa. (d) Energy = ½Fx = ½ × 100 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.05 J.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Sketch a typical stress–strain graph for a ductile metal such as copper, labelling the limit of proportionality, elastic limit, yield point and breaking point. Explain each region.', ['Graph shape', 'Limit of proportionality', 'Elastic limit', 'Yield and plastic region', 'Breaking point'], 'From the origin the graph is a straight line up to the limit of proportionality (Hooke\'s law obeyed). Just beyond is the elastic limit; up to here the wire returns to its original length when unloaded. Beyond the elastic limit, at the yield point, the wire stretches a lot for little extra stress (plastic deformation) and does not return to its original length. The curve rises to the ultimate tensile stress, then the wire necks and breaks at the breaking point.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Two identical springs, each of spring constant 40 N/m, are connected (a) in series, (b) in parallel, and a load of 4 N is hung from each arrangement. Find the extension in each case.', ['Series effective k', '0.2 m', 'Parallel effective k', '0.05 m'], '(a) In series, each spring carries 4 N and stretches 4 ÷ 40 = 0.1 m, so total extension = 0.2 m (effective k = 20 N/m). (b) In parallel, each spring carries 2 N and stretches 2 ÷ 40 = 0.05 m, so the extension = 0.05 m (effective k = 80 N/m).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain the terms elastic, plastic, ductile and brittle, giving an example of a material for each.', ['Elastic', 'Plastic', 'Ductile', 'Brittle'], 'Elastic: returns to its original shape when the deforming force is removed (steel spring, rubber band). Plastic: undergoes permanent deformation (plasticine, lead). Ductile: can be drawn into wires because it undergoes large plastic deformation before breaking (copper, gold). Brittle: breaks with little or no plastic deformation (glass, cast iron, chalk).', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 4],
