        ['The breeze that blows from the sea to the land during the day is a ___ breeze.', ['sea'], '', 'Land heats faster.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['Draw and label a vacuum flask and explain how each part reduces heat transfer.', ['Labelled diagram', 'Vacuum stops conduction and convection', 'Silvering reduces radiation', 'Stopper', 'Supports'], 'Labelled diagram: double-walled glass container with a vacuum between the walls, silvered inner surfaces, a cork or plastic stopper, insulating supports and an outer protective case. The vacuum prevents heat transfer by conduction and convection because there are no particles to carry heat. The silvered surfaces reflect radiant heat back (into hot liquids or away from cold ones), reducing radiation. The stopper, made of an insulator, reduces conduction and prevents convection and evaporation at the top. Insulating supports reduce conduction to the outer case.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe an experiment to compare the thermal conductivities of copper, iron and glass rods.', ['Equal rods', 'Wax or pins', 'Heat one end equally', 'Observation', 'Conclusion'], 'Take rods of equal length and thickness of the three materials. Coat each with a thin layer of wax (or stick drawing pins with wax at equal intervals). Fix one end of each rod into a container of boiling water (or heat equally with a flame). After some time, observe how far along each rod the wax has melted (or how many pins have fallen). The wax melts furthest along copper, then iron, then glass, showing that copper is the best conductor and glass the poorest.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain, using convection, the formation of land and sea breezes in coastal towns such as Accra and Cape Coast.', ['Land heats faster', 'Warm air rises', 'Sea breeze by day', 'Land cools faster at night', 'Land breeze'], 'During the day, the land heats up faster than the sea because it has a lower specific heat capacity. Air above the land is warmed, expands, becomes less dense and rises. Cooler, denser air from over the sea moves in to replace it, producing a sea breeze. At night the land cools faster than the sea; the air over the sea is now warmer and rises, and cooler air from the land flows out to sea, producing a land breeze.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe an experiment using a Leslie cube to show that dull black surfaces are better emitters of radiation than shiny surfaces.', ['Cube with different faces', 'Same temperature', 'Detector at equal distance', 'Readings compared', 'Conclusion'], 'A Leslie cube is a metal box whose four sides have different surfaces: dull black, shiny silver, white and dull grey. Fill it with hot water so that all faces are at the same temperature. Place a thermopile (or infrared thermometer) at the same distance from each face in turn and record the reading. The dull black face gives the largest reading and the shiny silver face the smallest, showing that dull black is the best emitter and shiny the worst.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Give three practical applications each of conduction and convection in everyday life and explain one of each.', ['Three conduction uses', 'Three convection uses', 'Explanations'], 'Conduction: metal cooking pots conduct heat to food; wooden handles and oven gloves are insulators; lagging pipes and roofs reduces heat loss; double glazing traps air. Convection: heating elements at the bottom of kettles; air conditioners and refrigerator freezer compartments placed at the top; chimneys and ventilators; sea and land breezes. Explanation, e.g. the freezer at the top of a fridge cools the air, which becomes denser and sinks, setting up a convection current that cools the whole fridge.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 2, 'theory', 6],
    ],
