        ['The colour of light refracted least by a prism is ___.', ['red'], '', 'Longest wavelength.', 2, 'fill'],
        ['List the members of the electromagnetic spectrum in order of increasing frequency and give one use and one hazard of any three of them.', ['Correct order', 'Three uses', 'Three hazards'], 'Order: radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays. Radio: broadcasting and communication; little hazard at normal levels. Microwaves: cooking, mobile phones and satellites; can heat body tissue. Infrared: remote controls, thermal imaging, heaters; can burn skin. Ultraviolet: sterilising, detecting forged notes; sunburn and skin cancer. X-rays: imaging bones, airport security; can damage cells and cause cancer. Gamma rays: cancer treatment, sterilising; cell damage and mutations.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 8],
        ['Describe how a pure spectrum of white light can be produced. Draw the arrangement and explain why the colours separate.', ['Narrow slit', 'Lens for parallel beam', 'Prism', 'Second lens focuses', 'Different refractive indices'], 'White light from a source passes through a narrow slit and a converging lens placed so that the slit is at its focus, producing a parallel beam. The beam passes through a prism set at minimum deviation, then a second converging lens focuses each colour onto a screen in a separate position, forming a pure spectrum with colours that do not overlap. The colours separate because the refractive index of glass is different for each wavelength: violet slows most and is deviated most, red least.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 8],
        ['Explain the appearance of (a) a red rose in white light, (b) a red rose in green light, (c) a white shirt in blue light, (d) a yellow cloth in red light.', ['Red in white', 'Black in green', 'Blue in blue', 'Red in red'], '(a) Red: the petals reflect red light and absorb the other colours. (b) Black: green light is absorbed and there is no red light to reflect. (c) Blue: a white surface reflects all colours that fall on it, so it reflects the blue light. (d) Red: yellow pigment reflects red and green; only red falls on it, so it appears red.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain how a rainbow is formed.', ['Refraction on entry', 'Dispersion', 'Internal reflection', 'Refraction on exit', 'Observer position'], 'Sunlight enters spherical raindrops and is refracted, with different colours bending by different amounts (dispersion). The light is totally (internally) reflected at the back of the drop and refracted again as it leaves, increasing the separation of the colours. Each colour emerges at a slightly different angle (about 40° to 42°), so an observer with the Sun behind sees red on the outside and violet on the inside of the arc.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
        ['Compare the properties of X-rays and gamma rays, and state two precautions taken by radiographers.', ['Similarities', 'Origin of X-rays', 'Origin of gamma rays', 'Two precautions'], 'Similarities: both are electromagnetic waves travelling at 3 × 10⁸ m/s; both are very penetrating, ionising and can damage living cells; both affect photographic film. Differences: X-rays are produced when fast electrons strike a metal target in an X-ray tube; gamma rays come from changes in the nuclei of radioactive atoms; gamma rays generally have higher frequency and energy. Precautions: radiographers stand behind lead screens; wear film badges to monitor exposure; limit exposure time; patients wear lead aprons over parts not being imaged.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
    ],
